    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d104">

                <pb facs="0218" n="114" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg114"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Mizner</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-10-02"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 2,
                        1890</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 188.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to instruction No. 186 of the
                    29th ultimo, I have to state that according to a communication of yesterday from
                    the Western Union Telegraph office here, it has been advised by the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Director Telegrafos</hi>, San Salvador, that the
                    telegram of July 20 last, in which you were instructed to demand instant release
                    of <hi rendition="#italic">Colima</hi> and cargo—and which you say in your No.
                    160 of the 10th ultimo never reached you—“was duly sent to its destination on
                    same day by way of Honduras, as direct communication with Guatemala was
                    impossible.”</p>
                <p>Awaiting the result of your own inquiries,</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James G. Blaine.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
